  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nNorthwest flow aloft will continue through Thursday, around the\nperiphery of a 595dam 500mb ridge centered over the desert\nsouthwest and southern high plains. We'll remain in an unsettled\npattern until the southwestern upper ridge expands into our\nregion this weekend, bringing hot and dry weather to the\nMidsouth.\n\nLate evening KSRX radar showed scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms just east of a shortwave over eastern OK. This\nactivity was moving southeast. Further east, modest cloud top\ncooling was noted on GOES IR over northern AR. Scattered light\nshowers may edge into eastern AR, southwest TN and northwest MS\ntoward morning. NBM thunder probs appear overdone given the weak\ninstability, and have been dialed back thunder in the gridded\nforecasts.\n\nThunderstorms appear on track for late Tuesday night through\nWednesday evening. The global models bring a shortwave trough\nthrough the Midsouth Wednesday morning, preceded by predawn\nshowers and a few thunderstorms. Most HREF members, including the\nHRRR, bring a second round of storms in from the northwest\nWednesday afternoon and evening. The upper features associated\nwith this second round appear to be more subtle, perhaps tied to\nan MCV and aided by daytime instability. REFS guidance depicts\nless coverage of storms Wednesday afternoon, relative to the\nHREF. In either case, there will be some question about MLCIN in\ncloud cover remains from storms earlier in the day. 00Z REFS\nlightning probs indicate a greater potential for additional\nstorms in the predawn hours Thursday. RRFS soundings depict CAPE\nnear 1000 J/kg rooted above a shallow surface inversion. This\nwill be monitored as additional guidance arrives.\n\nDry conditions appear in store in time for Thursday evening's\nlunar eclipse, though some clouds may remain over northeast MS.\n\nWe'll trade the unsettled weather of northwest flow for upper\nlevel ridging as we head into the weekend. By Sunday, the upper\nridge will flatten and expand eastward through the lower MS River\nValley, reaching heights of 595dam at 500mb over the Midsouth.\nDespite lower sun angle and shortened daylight in late August,\nhighs will prevail in the upper 90s over most of the Midsouth\nfrom Saturday through early next week.",
